#### Beam pipe scattering effect correction

For low \(p_{T}\) proton, there is some scattering contribution which comes from the beam pipe interaction. Obviously this effect has no contribution to the anti-particles like \(\bar{p}\). The protons which come from the beam pipe scattering have large dca. Therefore, we can use Eq. 3.4 to correct the low \(p_{T}\) proton spectra.

\[p\;yield=\frac{\bar{p}\;yield|_{dca<3cm}}{\bar{p}\;yield|_{dca<1cm}}\times p\;yield |_{dca<1cm} \tag{3.4}\]

### 3.4 Feed-down correction

#### Feed-down correction for pions

Weak-decay feed-down (e.g. \(K_{S}^{0}\rightarrow\pi^{+}\pi^{-}\)) to the pion spectra is calculated using the measured \(K_{S}^{0}\) and \(\Lambda\) spectra [13] and GEANT simulation. The feed-down contribution is subtracted from the pion spectra and found to be \(\sim 12\%\) at \(p_{T}=0.35\;GeV/c\), decreasing to \(\sim 5\%\) for \(p_{T}\geq 1\;GeV/c\).

Figure 3.12: TOF matching efficiency as a function of \(p_{T}\). The left panel is for \(p\) and the right panel is for \(\bar{p}\).

#### Feed-down correction for protons and anti-protons

The \(p\) and \(\bar{p}\) feed-down corrections are estimated using the \(\Lambda\) spectra from Ref. [13] with a full simulation of decay, detection efficiency, and momentum resolution. The measured \(\Lambda\) spectra are extrapolated to high \(p_{T}\) assuming \(\Lambda/p=0.2\) at \(p_{T}=10\) GeV/\(c\). The \(\Sigma^{+}/\Lambda\) ratio is assumed to be 0.35 [52], independent of \(p_{T}\). The systematic uncertainty on the correction is calculated from the statistical and systematic uncertainties on the inclusive proton and \(\Lambda\) measurements, with a 30% uncertainty assigned to the extrapolated \(\Lambda\) spectra. An additional 20% uncertainty is assigned to account for the uncertainty in the \(\Sigma^{+}\) yields.

At low \(p_{T}\) region, there is another method for \(\bar{p}\) feed-down correction independent of the \(\Lambda\) and \(\Sigma\) spectra measurement. The dca distribution of the primordial \(\bar{p}\) and the \(\bar{p}\) from weak decays are different. To derive the primordial \(\bar{p}\) yield, we can use these 2 dca distributions to fit the inclusive \(\bar{p}\) dca distribution by the lease \(\chi^{2}\) method.

Fig. 3.13 shows the dca fitting method in two \(p_{T}\) bins. Blue dashed lines and red dashdotted lines represent the simulated dca distributions for weak decays and primordial \(\bar{p}\), respectively. Green solid lines represent the sum of two simulated dca distributions and the black circles represent the inclusive \(\bar{p}\) dca distributions for real data.

