### 3.6 Reaction plane method for \(v_{2}\) calculation

In heavy ion collisions, the event plane is reconstructed from the detected final particle azimuths. The acceptance and efficiency of the detectors in azimuth is corrected by compensating the azimuth to a flat distribution with \(\Phi\) weights. Technically, the \(\Phi\) weights are created for different days to deal with the different situations in a long

Figure 3.15: Deuteron tracking efficiency as a function of \(p_{T}\). The left panel is for 0-10% centrality and the right panel is for 0-80% centrality.

running period. The second order harmonic event plane azimuth \(\Psi_{2}\) can be calculated from the \(\overrightarrow{Q}\) vector, as Eq. 3.7,3.8:

\[\Psi_{2}=\left(\arctan\frac{Q_{y}}{Q_{x}}\right)/2,\hskip 56.905512pt0<\Psi_{2}<\pi \tag{3.7}\]

\[\overrightarrow{Q}=\left(Q_{x},Q_{y}\right)=\left(\sum_{i}w_{i}\cdot\cos(2\phi _{i}),\hskip 14.226378pt\sum_{i}w_{i}\cdot\sin(2\phi_{i})\right) \tag{3.8}\]

Here, \(w_{i}\) is the weight for each track included in the event plane calculation, which includes both the \(\Phi\) weight and the \(p_{T}\) weight. Fig. 3.18 shows a \(\Phi\) weight distributions for a single day. The tracks selected in the event plane calculation should satisfy the criteria listed in Table. 3.3.

Fig. 3.18 shows a typical \(\Phi\) weight distribution. Fig. 3.19 shows the event plane angle distribution before and after the \(\Phi\) weight correction. It is clear that the \(\Phi\) weight correction is necessary.

The resolution of the event plane is calculated using the sub-event method [54]. Each event is divided into two sub-events with nearly equal multiplicity by 3 different

Figure 3.16: \(p\) tracking efficiency over \(\bar{p}\) tracking efficiency as a function of \(p_{T}\) in different centrality bins.

ways, random, the charge of tracks and the \(\eta\) of tracks. The event plane is reconstructed in each sub-event, denoted as \(\Psi_{2}^{a}\) and \(\Psi_{2}^{b}\). Then the event plane resolution \(r=\langle cos[2(\Psi_{2}-\Psi_{rp})]\rangle\) can be calculated from Eq.(14) and (11) from [54]:

\[\langle\cos[2(\Psi_{2}-\Psi_{rp})]\rangle=\frac{\sqrt{\pi}}{2\sqrt{2}}\chi_{2} \exp(-\chi_{2}^{2}/4)\times[I_{0}(\chi_{2}^{2}/4)+I_{1}(\chi_{2}^{2}/4)] \tag{3.9}\]

\[\langle\cos[2(\Psi_{2}^{a}-\Psi_{rp})]\rangle=\sqrt{\langle\cos[2(\Psi_{2}^{a} -\Psi_{2}^{b})]\rangle} \tag{3.10}\]

\[\chi_{2}=v_{2}/\sigma=v_{2}\sqrt{2N} \tag{3.11}\]

Firstly, we obtained the sub-event resolution \(\langle\cos[2(\Psi_{2}^{a}-\Psi_{rp})]\rangle\) from Eq. 3.10. Then Eq. 3.9 can be solved as an iterative routine to extract the sub-event \(\chi_{2}^{a}\). This variable is proportional to \(\sqrt{N}\) according to Eq. 3.11, so the total event \(\chi_{2}\) is obtained by \(\chi_{2}=\sqrt{2}\chi_{2}^{a}\). After putting this \(\chi_{2}\) into Eq. 3.9, we calculated the final full event resolution. The physical \(v_{2}\) is calculated as \(v_{2}=v_{2}^{obs}/r\), where \(v_{2}^{obs}\) is the observed

